Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Offsets: where a crease flattens to tangency at an end at which its faces
go on smoothly into a further face, the intersection of the offsets of its
faces is the branch along the crease alone, cut at the tangencies
(mitcad#2140, GitHub mitcad/Mitcad#517).

The case: a Y tube, a circle of radius 10 swept along a line of 25 and a
tangent quarter arc of radius 30, mirrored and joined: a cylinder below and
two tori, the arms, above. The arms meet along a concave crease (their
intersection in the mirror plane, two edges split where both tori's seams
pass, at the crotch's top) that fades to tangency at both its ends, where
the arms leave the cylinder; there the tori run on smoothly (G1) into the
cylinder along the circle where all three meet. Shelled inwards 2 mm with
the three ends removed, Intersection join: BRepOffset_Inter3d::
ConnexIntByInt intersects the enlarged offset tori (once for the face pair,
with the first of the crease's edges). They cross along an oval in the
mirror plane: from the crotch's top (z = 48.32) through the tangencies
(0, -8, 25) and (0, 8, 25), where both are tangent to the cylinder's
offset, and on below them to the enlarged faces' end (z = 9.71), two edges
split at the top (the trace below). DropCrossingsPastTangentEnd (patches
1130 and 1190) found the tangency on the plane of the circle and joined
the branch to its continuation (patch 1190's case of a removed end): the
thick solid raised an exception without a message (MitCad: "the geometry
kernel's offset raised an error"). Kept whole instead, the two edges run
past the tangencies into the cylinder's offset, and the inner wall of an
arm was a face whose wire does not close (an invalid shell).

- BRepOffset_Tool::Inter3D takes the analysis of the shape (an optional
  argument, null by default, which ConnexIntByInt now passes) and gives it
  to DropCrossingsPastTangentEnd.
- There, where the tangent end's vertex has an edge of RefFace1, other than
  the crease and not its seam, that the analysis finds tangential
  (ChFiDS_Tangential) between RefFace1 and a face other than RefFace2, the
  faces go on smoothly past the end: the walls go on as the offset of that
  face, which meets the crease's offset at the tangency. There the branch
  along the edge is not joined to its continuation. The branch (the edge
  nearest the crease's middle and those it goes on into, as past a
  solid's end, wherever the tangency lies) is cut at the tangencies of the
  crease's ends: of every edge common to both faces (the pair is
  intersected once for all of them), each end where the faces' normals are
  parallel (within 1e-4) and the faces go on smoothly, its point on the
  offset surface. An edge of the branch passing within four times patch
  1190's neighbourhood (20 % of the offset) of a tangency is cut at its
  point nearest it, and of its parts the one whose middle is nearest to
  the crease's edges is kept; an end not cut keeps the edge's own vertex
  (the branch's edges share it at the crotch's top), a cut end has a new
  one, and its curves on both offset faces are the edge's own, trimmed
  (on a periodic face the pave filler puts them on the seam's sides
  consistently; built afresh, each piece took a side of its own and an
  arm's wall closed into a loop across its seam without it). The pieces, with their curves on both offset
  faces, are the intersection (Inter3D's new list from
  DropCrossingsPastTangentEnd); where a piece's curve on either offset
  face cannot be built, the cut is not taken and the branch is kept whole
  (OrientSection reads both curves). Where no boundary at that end is curved
  across the edge, the direction out of the edge orders the branch's
  pieces. Ends at removed faces or sharp edges are handled as before.
- BRepOffset_Inter3d::ConnexIntByInt: the face pair is intersected once,
  with the first of its edges, and every edge of the pair got all the
  pieces; Inter2d, at the crease's vertex where the face's seam goes on,
  then met the seam with the wrong piece and the arm's inner wall closed
  across its seam without it (the seam's offset dropped). Where several
  edges share the pair's pieces and they run along different edges, each
  edge keeps the pieces whose middles are nearest to it (an edge that would
  keep none keeps them all, as before), and a piece no longer counts as
  coming from the edges it was dropped from.
  (First run: the shell of OCCT's own Y tube, geometry.port.2140, is built
  and right; on MitCad's mirrored tube the left arm's wall is now whole,
  the right one lost its seam instead.)
- BRepOffset_Inter2d::ConnexIntByInt walks each face's wire on the face
  oriented forwards, the edges' orientations those in it, but intersected
  the images on the offset face as oriented: BRep_Tool::CurveOnSurface
  swaps a seam's two curves on a reversed face, so on a reversed face (a
  mirrored body's arm) the seam's curve on the other side of the period was
  intersected with the crease piece before it (the piece at v = 0, the
  seam's curve at v = 2 pi, and the other way round), no vertex came of
  it, and that arm's inner wall lost its seam (the trace's second run;
  OCCT's own Y tube, both arms forward, was right). Where a seam takes
  part, the images are intersected on the offset face oriented forwards,
  as the wire is walked; elsewhere nothing changes (an edge's curve on a
  face does not depend on the face's orientation but for a seam's).
- With MITCAD_TRACE_OFFSET_CREASE set, BRepOffset_Inter2d::ConnexIntByInt
  says, where a face's seam meets an intersected edge, the two edges it
  intersects (their ends, curves on the face and orientations) and whether
  a vertex came of it.
- With MITCAD_TRACE_OFFSET_CREASE set (read each time),
  DropCrossingsPastTangentEnd says on the standard error the crease's
  ends and the sines of its faces' angle there, the pieces of the
  intersection, whether the end is smooth, the branch it takes and what
  it keeps or cuts (for the diagnosis; nothing changes otherwise).

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2140.
